\section{Coupled fields and spatially-varying trends}{

The spatial engine (\code{method = "nested_laplace"}, default
\code{control$engine = "joint_coupled"}) couples one shared areal field (the
cell intercept, an unweighted \code{icar()} / \code{bym2()} term) across the occupancy
and cover arms. ADDITIONAL coupled fields - spatially-varying coefficients,
e.g. a temporal trend - are added as \emph{weighted} areal terms in the formula:

\if{html}{\out{<div class="sourceCode r">}}\preformatted{~ elev + icar(graph = adj) + icar(graph = adj, weight = year)
}\if{html}{\out{</div>}}

Each weighted term \code{icar(graph, weight = col)} is a second coupled field
whose contribution to a predictor row is \code{weight_i * z[cell_i]}. All coupled
fields share the same graph; each couples onto the cover arm with its own
scale (\code{alpha} for the intercept field, \code{alpha_trend} for a trend field),
integrated over the outer grid. The intercept field is reported in
\code{fit$spatial_field}, trend fields in \code{fit$trend_field} / \code{fit$trend_fields}.
The trend coupling grid defaults to \code{control$alpha.grid}; override it with
\code{control$alpha.grid.trend}.

A single trend field may also be requested out-of-band with
\code{control = list(trend = list(weight = "<col>"))}, naming a numeric per-cell
covariate in the cell \code{data}; this is the equivalent of one weighted formula
term. Specify the trend field one way or the other, not both.
}

\section{Sites larger than cells (\code{group_var})}{

By default each site (one row of \code{y} / \code{data}, one latent occupancy state) is
its own field node, so the graph must have one node per site. Passing
\code{group_var = "<col>"} to the \code{icar()} / \code{bym2()} term maps each site to a
field node named by that integer column, so several sites can share one node.
The field then stays length \code{n_cells} (the graph) while occupancy, detection,
and cover run over \code{n_sites}. The motivating layout is a site = cell x
time-period: plots in a cell-period are the detection replicates, occupancy is
per cell-period, and a per-site time weight on a coupled trend field
(\code{icar(graph, weight = time, group_var = "cell")}) gives a detection-corrected
occupancy trend on a shared cell field. Supported on the default
\code{joint_coupled} engine; the \code{v2_joint} / \code{v3_nested} escape hatches bind the
field 1:1 to sites and reject \code{group_var}.
}

\section{Per-group random intercept on the shared-field path (\code{re()} / \code{(1 | g)})}{

On the spatial \code{nested_laplace} path a single random INTERCEPT term on the psi
formula -- \code{re(g)} or the \code{(1 | g)} bar -- layers a per-group occupancy offset
on top of the shared field. It joins the joint fit as one \code{iid} prior block
whose latent rides the occupancy arm only; its variance integrates on the
outer grid alongside the field sigma / alpha and is reported as the \code{sigma_re}
hyperparameter, with the per-group BLUPs in \code{fit$re} and via \code{\link[=ranef]{ranef()}}. A
default \code{re.sigma.grid} (log-spaced) sets the grid; pass \code{control$re.sigma.grid}
to override. Scope: one random-intercept term (a scalar per group maps onto the
one iid block); a random slope, a correlated multi-coefficient block, or an RE
without a shared field is rejected (the joint engine integrates every variance
component on its grid, so multiple variance components do not scale -- richer
RE is the non-spatial cover-hurdle EM's route). This is the single-species
analogue of the community spatial occu_cover (\code{\link[=ms_occu_cover]{ms_occu_cover()}}, gcol33/tulpa#67)
and the tulpaObs consumer of tulpa's field + per-group RE engine composition
